{{~##
 Every record type as an incomplete type, in one header.

 This is where C's answer to the cross-reference problem lives. A record holding a whole-row
 reference has a member of type `const OtherRecord_t*`, and a pointer needs only an
 incomplete type - so no table header has to include another, and two tables pointing at
 each other is not an include cycle.

 In one header rather than each table header declaring what it needs, because a repeated
 `typedef struct X X;` in one translation unit is a constraint violation in C99 - legal in
 C11 and accepted by every compiler either way, which is exactly the kind of thing that
 compiles for years and then does not. Declared once here, included by all of them.

 The encryption key is here for a reason that follows from the same layout. It belongs to the
 model rather than to any one table, and the natural place to say so would be the accessor
 header - but that one includes the table headers rather than the other way round, so a table
 source that included it would be reaching back through the whole model for two symbols. This
 is the one header every generated file already has, so the declaration goes here and the
 definition beside the accessor's own load.
##~}}
{{ include 'c-header-head.sbn' ~}}
{{~ for line in records ~}}
typedef struct {{ line }} {{ line }};
{{~ end ~}}

/* The key the table files were sealed with, and its length - NULL and 0 when they were not
 * sealed.
 *
 * Set once by the consuming project, at start-up, before the first table is read, rather
 * than passed to every Load: a key threaded through every load signature would be the same
 * value written out at every call site, and every call site is somewhere it could be wrong.
 * The bytes are not copied, so whatever they point at has to outlive the loading.
 *
 * Thirty-two bytes, the key the converter was given. NULL - the default - is a project that
 * does not encrypt, and it costs that project nothing: the load path opens the envelope
 * either way, and a file that is not encrypted comes back from that unchanged. A project
 * that does encrypt and leaves this NULL gets the reader's own message saying the file is
 * encrypted and no key was given, rather than a failure somewhere inside the format.
 *
 * Where the key comes from is the one thing about this layer worth being careful with. It is
 * a secret only until it reaches a repository, a build log or an issue, and it cannot be
 * taken back afterwards - so it is read at start-up from wherever this project keeps
 * secrets, not written into a source file as an array literal. What the layer is for, and
 * therefore what is and is not worth defending, is spec/wire/tcb-v104-composed-encodings.md
 * section 4. */
extern const uint8_t* {{ accessor.name }}_EncryptionKey;
extern int32_t {{ accessor.name }}_EncryptionKeyLength;

/* The key the table files were signed with, or NULL when they were not signed.
 *
 * A different key from the encryption one and independent of it: a file can be signed
 * without being encrypted, and encrypted without being signed.
 *
 * What it buys is the thing encryption does not. The cipher is a keystream XOR, so a bit
 * flipped in the file is a bit flipped in the value it decrypts to - no key needed - and
 * every structural check the format makes still passes, because a four-byte float accepts
 * any four bytes. With this set, a file altered after export stops loading instead.
 *
 * Setting it makes the reader stricter in a second way: a file that carries no MAC is
 * refused, because otherwise the check could be removed by zeroing sixteen bytes. So the
 * order to turn this on in is data first, key second. spec/wire/tcb-mac-and-signature.md. */
extern const uint8_t* {{ accessor.name }}_MacKey;
extern int32_t {{ accessor.name }}_MacKeyLength;

/* Whether to check the MAC at all. True by default.
 *
 * For tools that read exported files without the key, and for measuring what the check costs
 * at load. It is not a security boundary - anyone who can flip a flag in a shipped binary can
 * read the key out of the same binary. */
extern bool {{ accessor.name }}_VerifyMac;
{{ include 'c-header-foot.sbn' ~}}
